# Licence

This repository contains two kinds of material with different owners. Read both parts
before reusing anything.

## 1. This repository's own contribution — CC BY 4.0

Copyright (c) 2026 Kostadin Kostadinov

The following are the original contribution of this repository and are licensed under the
Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International licence (CC BY 4.0):

- the structure and organisation of the files, including the per-item block format
- the YAML frontmatter schema (version, DOI, `applies_to`, `extends`, `supersedes`,
  `licence`, `licence_basis`, provenance fields)
- the routing guidance — which guideline applies to which study design
- the cross-references between guidelines, and the notes explaining how extensions layer
  onto their parent checklists
- the version warnings and supersession notes
- the editorial commentary in each file (the prose above the items)
- `README.md`, `INVENTORY.md`, and the compilation of `references.bib`

Full licence text: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode

## 2. The checklist item text — owned by the guideline developers

The reporting-guideline items themselves are **not** the property of this repository and
are **not** licensed by it. Each remains subject to the terms of its own developers and
publisher.

Every guideline file records:

- `licence:` — the licence, where established, or an explicit statement that it is not verified
- `licence_basis:` — how that determination was made
- `doi:` and `official_url:` — so the authoritative source can always be reached

See `NOTICE` for the per-guideline position.

**Item text is reproduced here for accurate reference in research reporting, with full
attribution and a citable DOI in every case.** Several guidelines are explicitly
CC-licensed. Others have not yet been verified and are marked as such — treat those as
all-rights-reserved until confirmed. If you are a guideline developer and object to your
checklist appearing here, open an issue and it will be removed or reduced to metadata and
a link.

## 3. What this repository is not

A working aid, not an authoritative copy. Where a checklist matters — a submission, a peer
review, a compliance statement — use the official source. Transcription errors are possible
and are bugs worth reporting.
